  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 136 PM CDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nFor the rest of the day, broad surface high pressure building \nacross the Upper Midwest will maintain quiet, sunny, and \nunseasonably mild conditions across the Northland. Current RH \nvalues are already dipping into the low 30s and upper 20s \npercent, further supporting the lowering of minimum RH each day \nfor the past few days as very dry air aloft continues to mix \ndown toward the surface, keeping daytime humidity exceptionally \nlow. Clear skies and light winds tonight will promote efficient\nradiational cooling, bringing a chilly night across the area,\nthough not as cold as last night.\n\nOn Tuesday, a stable omega blocking pattern becomes firmly\nestablished across the central United States, keeping our region\nunder the control of an upper-level ridge. Kept the trend for \nslightly increased daily maximum temperatures going each day \nthrough Thursday. Plentiful sunshine will prevail alongside \nlight southerly breezes. Meanwhile, local lake breezes will \ndevelop along the Lake Superior shoreline during the afternoon, \nkeeping coastal areas noticeably cooler and closer to seasonal \nnorms along the North Shore, while interior communities \ncontinue to experience unseasonable autumn warmth. The lake\nbreeze is expected to move the most inland on Tuesday and\nThursday.\n\nChanges begin on Friday as the persistent omega ridge starts to\nbreak down. A closed upper-level low over the Great Basin will eject\nnortheastward toward the Upper Midwest. Cloud cover will \nsteadily increase throughout the day. Rain chances are still on \ntrack to arrive Friday night into Saturday, and confidence is \nhigher that precipitation will occur over that time period even \nif scattered as models are becoming more constant with that\ntiming. Because temperatures ahead of the system remain \nunseasonably mild, all precipitation will fall strictly as \nliquid rain.\n\nScattered light rain showers will linger across the area \nthrough Saturday as the trough axis and associated cold front \npush across the Upper Great Lakes. By Sunday, dry conditions \nreturn as high pressure rebuilds behind the departing system, \naccompanied by clearing skies. A significant air mass change \nbehind the cold front will drop temperatures back toward \nseasonal values for late October.",
